10:46:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your instructor’s advice is good: pull up to the marked stopping position / sensor area so the signal can detect your car. “Feel the weight” is a common teaching shorthand because it conveys the right behavior. In many places, though, the pavement “pad” is an inductive detector rather than a literal pressure or weight pad. It senses the vehicle’s metal electrically, not its downward force. There have also been genuine pressure- or axle-weight-based roadway detectors, especially in older or specialized installations, so the exact hardware can vary by location. Either way, the practical rule is the same: - Stop at or just behind the stop line, where indicated. - If visible loop cuts are present, position the vehicle over them rather than far past or before them. - Don’t creep into the intersection trying to make the light change. - If a signal clearly fails to detect you, follow local rules for malfunctioning signals or report it to the road authority. So your instructor was not wrong about how to drive; the technical distinction is mostly about what type of sensor is under the road.
