10:46:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That pattern is real, but it still does not prove the detector is measuring weight. A large truck is usually detected more reliably and sometimes sooner because it has: - much more steel in its frame, engine, axles, wheels, and body; - a larger metal area over the detection loop; - a longer vehicle body, often covering multiple detector zones; - a stronger signature for radar/camera systems as well. An inductive-loop detector can be set to trigger when the signal change crosses a threshold. A truck produces a much larger electrical change than a small car, so it may cross that threshold immediately. A small car may produce a weaker change, especially if it stops in a less sensitive part of the loop or the detector is poorly calibrated. Also, what looks like “the light took a second to notice the car” can be caused by the **signal controller**, not the sensor. The controller may wait for a minimum green time on the cross street, a pedestrian phase, clearance timing, coordination with nearby lights, or confirmation from another detection zone. It generally cannot switch to green instantly merely because a vehicle arrived. If it were a true weight sensor, a loaded truck versus the same truck empty would make a major difference. With a typical inductive loop, their detection is nearly the same because the amount of metal has changed very little, even though their weight may differ by many tons. So: heavier trucks often trigger more reliably, but usually because they are **bigger and more metallic**, not because the pavement is functioning like a scale.
