10:48:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not in this case. A sensor can react to a car’s **metal mass** without reacting to its **physical mass/weight**. For example, an inductive loop is affected by electrically conductive material near it. A car’s steel body, engine, wheels, and frame alter the loop’s magnetic field. If you loaded the same car with 1,000 lb of cargo, its weight would increase substantially, but the loop’s response would change little or not at all because its metal content and position stayed nearly the same. Conversely, a large aluminum or steel object can strongly affect a loop even if it is not exerting much force on the road. So: - **Weight sensor:** responds to downward force/axle load. - **Inductive loop:** responds to conductive metal changing an electromagnetic field. “Mass” can mean several things in everyday speech, which makes this confusing, but an inductive loop is not measuring the car’s mass in the physics sense either. It is measuring an electrical-property change caused by nearby metal.
